{"type": "thinking", "text": "The study screen updated to light theme without restart. Good.\n\nNow, note the card reset to question (Show answer), the answer buttons hidden. The state wasn't preserved across leaving to Settings, but that's expected (new card resets?). Actually, we hadn't answered, so it's fine.\n\nNow let's test the answer buttons more thoroughly. Show answer, tap an answer, then check next card. Also verify intervals in a \"learning\" state. Let's do this carefully so we can still browse cards later.\n\nPlan for answer buttons:\n- Show answer → tap Easy on comer. Then note next card's intervals. Then perhaps use undo to revert? There's an Undo (the back arrow icon at top, element 1 at 784,146). Undo would revert the answer, restoring the card. That tests reversal and keeps data intact.\n\nActually, using Undo is a good way to test \"reversal\" for answer buttons. Let's do:\n1. Show answer, note intervals (1m,6m,10m,4d).\n2. Tap Easy (4d). \n3. Observe next card (e.g., agua) and its intervals.\n4. Undo (top back arrow) to revert; verify comer reappears and counts restored.\n\nBut the changed behavior is about intervals being computed by backend scheduler. To detect a bug, we might compare intervals between two cards of same type. All 4 Spanish are Basic new. Intervals should be identical initially. Hmm, unless some cards were already reviewed? Preconditions say 4 Basic notes, 4 due, 0 learn, 0 review. So all new. Buttons should all show same intervals for each card.\n\nWait, but there might be a bug where the button intervals don't update when moving to the next card (stale from previous backend call). To test, we could answer a card with Easy and see the next card's button intervals — they should be same for new cards. That wouldn't reveal stale values.\n\nAlternatively, after answering one card Easy, that card is no longer new. Then if we undo and re-answer... hmm.\n\nLet me think about how the bug might manifest. \"the interval shown on each button is now computed directly by the backend scheduler.\" Perhaps the app used to compute display intervals from a default; now from scheduler. A bug: the intervals shown don't match actual scheduling after answering. To verify, answer Easy (says 4d) and then check the card's due date via card info / browser to see if it's 4 days out. That would detect mismatch.\n\nLet's do that:\n1. Show answer → note Easy 4d.\n2. Tap Easy.\n3. Verify card leaves new queue; counts change (4 new -> 3 new, 1 review?).\n4. Check via card browser or statistics? Card browser can show due date.\n\nActually, we need to test the card browser anyway. We can check comer's due date there. But the card browser may show \"Due\" column as a date.\n\nAlternatively, use the \"Undo\" then check.\n\nLet me proceed with the study screen: show answer, then tap Easy. First screenshot to record intervals for this card. We already saw them for comer. Let's show answer again."}
