{"type": "thinking", "text": "System BACK also exits search mode and clears the query (4 cards shown). Hmm. So exiting the search bar always clears the search. That's probably standard AnkiDroid behavior in this build: the search bar is a transient query input.\n\nHmm wait — actually, in AnkiDroid, the card browser \"search\" becomes a filter in the toolbar; exiting search mode could clear it. But I recall the search persists (the filtered results remain, and the header shows \"1 card shown\"). Let me think. In AnkiDroid 2.16+, the card browser has a search bar at the top; pressing back collapses the keyboard (first back), then the search bar maybe collapses with query retained? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, the snackbar \"1 card shown\" is the \"subtitle\" referenced in the change. The changed behavior says \"Card browser header: the subtitle is now updated only when a search finishes.\" Maybe the subtitle refers to the text under \"Card browser\" title when the search is in progress, e.g., \"Searching…\" vs \"1 card shown\". Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the header subtitle is the \"N cards shown\" in the toolbar (which we see only when not in search mode). In the current build, exiting search clears the query, so we never see \"1 card shown\" in the header. That may itself be a bug — but is it the changed area? \"subtitle is now updated only when a search finishes\" — this suggests a regression where the subtitle updates on every keystroke (showing intermediate counts) instead of only after search finishes. To test, we need to observe the subtitle while typing in the search bar. But while in search mode, the subtitle isn't visible... unless the search bar is the \"expanded\" version and there's a subtitle below it.\n\nWait, maybe I'm using a different card browser UI than the \"header subtitle\" refers to. Perhaps there's a setting \"New study screen\"? Hmm no.\n\nLet me look more carefully. In AnkiDroid 2.17, the card browser has a toolbar with \"Card browser\" title and subtitle \"X cards\". There's a search bar. When you search, the subtitle updates. Actually, the layout `toolbar_content` shows \"Spanish\" | \"4 cards shown\" — that's the deck name and count. The count is likely the \"subtitle\".\n\nMaybe the search is supposed to persist: when you press the collapse arrow, it should collapse the search input but keep the search. Our observation shows the query cleared. Hmm, but maybe pressing \"Collapse\" always clears; maybe that's the bug? Wait, the collapse arrow is described \"Collapse\" — maybe collapsing the search bar clears? Hmm.\n\nLet me try a different route: press the \"Search all decks\" snackbar action. That will search all decks for \"comer\" and keep the query. Let me see if the header then shows \"1 card shown\" (subtitle). But the snackbar might have disappeared after the back press. Let me redo the search and tap \"Search all decks\".\n\nActually, an important thing: the changed build item is \"Card browser header: the subtitle is now updated only when a search finishes.\" This likely refers to a bug fix: previously the subtitle updated whenever the search text changed (on each keystroke) rather than when the search actually completed. The bug we might find is that the subtitle still updates prematurely or doesn't update at all. Since the subtitle is only visible when not in search mode (and search clears), maybe I need to find a way to keep a search active while seeing the toolbar.\n\nLet me check if there's a saved search / filter or if the browser has \"deck\" dropdown. The toolbar_content \"Spanish\" is clickable — maybe tapping it shows the deck list filter. The search might be persisted if we don't collapse.\n\nAlternative: Rotate the screen while a search is active! Rotation recreates the activity; maybe the query is retained and the subtitle visible. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the subtitle is shown in the search-mode toolbar too: under the search field? Let me look at the screenshot of search mode: the toolbar was blue with the search field. No subtitle. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about the actual AnkiDroid code. In CardBrowser, `mCardsCount` subtitle? There's a `updateSubtitle()` in the activity that sets the subtitle of the toolbar. It's called when the search is done (from the search task's onPostExecute) — that's what \"subtitle is now updated only when a search finishes\" refers to. The subtitle text is like \"123 cards shown\". The toolbar subtitle is shown under the title. In the old build, the subtitle might get updated in onTextChanged, showing stale/wrong counts.\n\nTo test this UI, we need the toolbar with title (not in search mode). The search in AnkiDroid might be initiated via the \"Search\" button which expands an inline search view; when submitted by pressing Enter, the search view could collapse automatically? We saw the snackbar. Hmm, but the toolbar remained search mode.\n\nWait — actually maybe the collapse button does NOT clear the query; maybe pressing it re-collapses to the *deck filter* and the list showed 4 because the search was actually cancelled. Hmm.\n\nLet me try: after search completes (1 card shown), press the system back key ONCE — maybe first back dismisses the keyboard and collapses the search view, keeping the query? We saw it cleared. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the issue is that the app has a bug: searching and then collapsing loses the search. But that's not the changed area (or is it? \"subtitle updated only when a search finishes\" — if search resets, subtitle can't update).\n\nHold on. Perhaps there is a different browser header: When you tap the deck name \"Spanish\" (toolbar_content), a dropdown appears listing decks; the subtitle \"4 cards shown\" is in the toolbar. The subtitle updates only after a search finishes. Let me try a search and then, without collapsing, rotate the screen. Rotation might show the toolbar state with the query and subtitle. Let me test rotation while search active.\n\nActually, let me test whether the \"subtitle\" is visible during search via the toolbar title area: In the search-mode screenshot, the title is the search field. There's no room for subtitle.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the card browser can be reached in a \"two-pane\" mode on tablets, but here phone.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole thing: The \"snackbar\" showing \"1 card shown\" — maybe that's the \"header subtitle\" they mean? It appears at bottom, not header. Hmm, but it says \"1 card shown\" which is similar to the subtitle \"4 cards shown\". The snackbar appears when a search finishes and the search is restricted to the current deck (\"Search all decks\" action). That's a known AnkiDroid feature: when you search in a deck, a snackbar is shown with count and \"Search all decks\". So the header subtitle \"4 cards shown\" is separate.\n\nMaybe the subtitle is updated only when the search finishes, and we can observe it by using a search that doesn't clear when collapsing: e.g., use the \"filter deck\" by tapping the deck name and selecting a deck? That's not a search.\n\nLet me try: start search mode, type \"comer\", press Search, then instead of collapsing, press the \"Search all decks\" snackbar action. That might keep the query and show all decks. Then the header may show \"1 card shown\". Let's try.\n\nActually, the snackbar fades after ~4s. Need to be quick.\n\nLet me redo: tap Search icon (empty), type \"comer\", tap keyboard Search, then quickly tap \"Search all decks\" (element 10). Then observe header.\n\nHmm, but tapping \"Search all decks\" might navigate to a new browser with the query. Let me try.\n\nActually, wait. Let me think about how to reliably see the toolbar subtitle with a search applied. Another approach: use the browser's \"Filter\" advanced search? Hmm.\n\nMaybe the search persists if you press the overflow \"Done\"? Not sure.\n\nLet me look at how AnkiDroid's browser search works in this version. The elements: when search bar open: AutoCompleteTextView \"Search\" id=search_src_text, Collapse arrow, Clear query X, More options. When collapsed: toolbar_content \"Spanish\" | \"4 cards shown\", Add, Search, More options. So the search view is an \"action view\" in the toolbar. Collapsing it (back arrow or system back) exits search mode. In AnkiDroid, collapsing the search view triggers `onQueryTextChange(\"\")` and maybe clears. Hmm.\n\nBut I recall that in", "truncated": true}
